\chapter{VMWeave, O1VM artifacts, and the runner}
\label{ch:83}
\index{VMWeave, O1VM artifacts, and the runner}
\index{MIR!O1VM backend}

The VM backend lowers admitted verified MIR to an O1VM artifact consumed by the VM runner. The repository includes a VMWeave specification and generated snapshots. Bytecode decoding has bounds and type checks independently of the source frontend. The VM profile adds specified unmanaged product layouts such as records, sums, arrays and slices. Its supported closure and foreign operations are limited by the guest representation and wire contract.

\section{Separate producer and consumer}
Emit one artifact, run it with the installed runner, and compare its results with a native build on a common source fixture. Exercise a truncated or malformed artifact separately to check decoder failure. Bytecode availability on one build does not prove platform-independent behavior for extensions that call native providers. When source diagnostic paths matter, record the configured source prefix mappings. Inspect the VM manual rather than treating every feature named in the source capability matrix as available in the guest.

\section{Decode once for inspection and again to execute}
Use the scalar trap fixture to predict the runner's observable result:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn mark(x:i64)->i64 { println(x); return x; }
fn main() { println(mark(7)/mark(0)); println(99); }
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Emit it with \OnxCodeTerm{--emit-vm} and pass the artifact first to
\OnxCodeTerm{on1x-vm --verify}. The zero divisor does not invalidate
the bytecode. Both operands have the required type, the division has
its checked semantics, and there is a valid control path to the final
print. Verification does not execute either call. Run the same file
without the verification switch: the two calls print seven and zero
in that order, the division faults with status 70, and neither the
quotient nor 99 is printed. Compare the failing operator's location
with the MIR evaluator on the same source.

This separates three uses of the artifact. Its magic and version identify
a candidate encoding; decoding and verification check typed instructions
and edges before they become runnable tape; execution gives those
instructions values in a guest frame. Truncating the file tests the second
stage and cannot serve as a test of arithmetic failure. Reducing the
standalone runner's step budget tests another execution outcome, exhaustion
with status 75, rather than division semantics.

For scalar source also emit canonical MIR, then emit VM bytes from that
saved MIR. The VM source test checks equality with the original unoptimized
artifact. Do not require that round trip for a VM-only product or closure
fixture: its source route can perform checked conversion that ordinary
scalar MIR source lowering does not admit.

